-- title:
--   Eigenvalue one on inertia at q forces an invariant vector
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a noetherian local domain and let $\rho$ be an adic Galois representation over $A$ in the sense of the project's structure [`GaloisRepAdic`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L16): a type $V$ with an $A$-module structure that is free and finite with $\operatorname{rank}_A V = 2$, together with a monoid homomorphism $\rho.\rho$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q) = \overline{\mathbb Q} \simeq_{\mathbb Q} \overline{\mathbb Q}$ (for $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ the algebraic closure `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) to the $A$-linear endomorphisms of $V$, subject to adic continuity: for every $n$ there is an intermediate field $L$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$, finite-dimensional over $\mathbb Q$, such that every $\sigma$ fixing $L$ pointwise satisfies $\rho(\sigma)v - v \in \mathfrak m^n \cdot V$ for all $v \in V$, where $\mathfrak m$ is the maximal ideal of $A$. Let $q$ be a prime number whose image in $A$ is a unit, and let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a nonunit of $P$. Write $I_P$ for the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb Q$, realised as the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup inside the decomposition subgroup of $P$. Assume that for every $\sigma \in I_P$ the value $1$ is a root of the characteristic polynomial of the endomorphism $\rho(\sigma)$ of $V$. Then there is a vector $v \in V$ with $v \neq 0$ and $\rho(\sigma)v = v$ for all $\sigma \in I_P$.
--
--   The statement upgrades a pointwise eigenvalue condition at a place $P$ above a prime $q$ invertible in the coefficient ring to the existence of a common inertia-invariant vector, using the structure of the inertia group at $q$ (its wild part and the tame quotient) rather than any property of the abstract group generated by the $\rho(\sigma)$. It is used in the treatment of the local behaviour at $q$ of the two-dimensional adic representations attached to primitive forms, in particular in the results producing an inertia-invariant vector, or an inertia eigenbasis at a prime exactly dividing the conductor.
